<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Task Management</span>
      <h1>Todoist vs TickTick: Which Task Manager Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Todoist and TickTick are the two task managers most often shortlisted by solo users and small teams in 2026, and they take very different approaches. Todoist wins on natural language input, team collaboration, and a clean minimal interface. TickTick wins on built-in extras like calendar, habit tracking, Pomodoro, and time tracking at a lower price. This comparison breaks down features, pricing, platform support, and real-world workflows so you can pick the right one.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://todoist.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Todoist Free</a>
      <a href="https://ticktick.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try TickTick Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Todoist</th><th>TickTick</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Quick capture, teams, minimalists</td><td>All-in-one solo productivity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>5 projects, 5 users per project</td><td>Unlimited tasks, limited lists</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pro Plan Price</td><td>$4/mo (Pro), $6/user/mo (Business)</td><td>$2.99/mo (Premium)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Natural Language Input</td><td>Best in class</td><td>Good, less reliable on edge cases</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Calendar View</td><td>Yes (Pro and Business)</td><td>Yes, more full-featured</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Habit Tracker</td><td>No native support</td><td>Yes, built-in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pomodoro Timer</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, integrated</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Time Tracking</td><td>No native support</td><td>Yes, built-in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kanban Boards</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Team Collaboration</td><td>Strong (Business plan)</td><td>Limited (shared lists only)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Integrations</td><td>80+ via integrations</td><td>Fewer, mostly calendar sync</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI Features</td><td>AI Assist (Pro and above)</td><td>Limited</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile Apps</td><td>iOS, Android, Wear OS, Apple Watch</td><td>iOS, Android, Wear OS, Apple Watch</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Offline Mode</td><td>Limited</td><td>Full offline support</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Todoist?</h2>
    <p>Todoist is a task manager built by Doist, founded in 2007 by Amir Salihefendic. It pioneered natural language task input, where typing "Meeting with Jane every Friday at 2pm #work" creates a recurring task with the correct due date, time, and project in a single line. This focus on fast, frictionless capture has made Todoist the default task manager for millions of users and the standard against which every other task app is measured.</p>
    <p>The current version supports projects, sections, labels, filters, Kanban boards, a calendar view added in 2024, and AI Assist for generating sub-tasks and breaking down large tasks. Todoist is available on every major platform including web, iOS, Android, macOS, Windows, Linux, Wear OS, and Apple Watch. The free tier allows 5 projects and 5 collaborators per project. Pro at $4 per month adds unlimited projects, reminders, calendar view, and AI Assist. Business at $6 per user per month adds team shared projects, admin roles, and audit logs.</p>

    <h2>What Is TickTick?</h2>
    <p>TickTick is a task manager built by Appest Inc, launched in 2013 by a team that previously built GTasks for Android. TickTick positions itself as an all-in-one productivity app, combining tasks, calendar, habit tracker, Pomodoro timer, and time tracking in a single product. The philosophy is that most users do not want to juggle five separate apps for tasks, habits, and focus work, so TickTick bundles them all together.</p>
    <p>The current version supports lists, folders, tags, Kanban boards, a full calendar view with drag-and-drop rescheduling, a habit tracker with streaks, a Pomodoro timer integrated with tasks, and a time tracker that logs actual work time per task. The free tier allows unlimited tasks but limits the number of lists, habits, and calendar widgets. Premium at $2.99 per month billed annually unlocks unlimited everything plus widgets, custom themes, and full calendar integration. TickTick is available on web, iOS, Android, macOS, Windows, Linux, and Wear OS.</p>

    <h2>Task Capture and Natural Language Input</h2>
    <p>How fast you can get a task out of your head and into the app defines the entire experience. Both apps support natural language input, but Todoist is meaningfully better at it.</p>

    <h3>Todoist Natural Language</h3>
    <p>Todoist's parser is the most reliable in the category. It handles complex recurrences like "every first Friday of the month", durations like "ev day at 9am for 2 hours", and project assignment with the # symbol. The parser also supports multiple languages including English, Spanish, French, German, and Chinese. For users who capture tasks from many sources, Todoist's input speed is unmatched.</p>

    <h3>TickTick Natural Language</h3>
    <p>TickTick supports similar natural language syntax, but the parser is less reliable on edge cases. Complex recurrences sometimes fail, and time-zone handling is weaker than Todoist. TickTick compensates with voice input on mobile and a quick capture bar widget on Android, which is faster than Todoist's mobile capture for users who prefer voice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Todoist</span> wins natural language input.</p>

    <h2>Calendar and Time Blocking</h2>
    <p>A calendar view turns a task list into a plan for the day. TickTick has had a full calendar view for years, while Todoist only added one in 2024 and it is still catching up.</p>

    <h3>TickTick Calendar</h3>
    <p>TickTick's calendar is the best of any task manager in 2026. It supports monthly, weekly, and daily views, drag-and-drop rescheduling, time blocking where you assign specific time slots to tasks, and a dedicated agenda view that shows the day's plan at a glance. It also integrates with Google Calendar and Outlook, so external events appear alongside tasks.</p>

    <h3>Todoist Calendar</h3>
    <p>Todoist's calendar view, added in 2024, supports monthly and weekly grids with drag-and-drop rescheduling. It integrates with Google Calendar and Outlook Calendar. However, it lacks time blocking and the dedicated agenda view that TickTick offers. For users who plan their day hour by hour, Todoist's calendar feels limited.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">TickTick</span> wins calendar and time blocking.</p>

    <h2>Habits and Pomodoro</h2>
    <p>Productivity is not just about tasks. Habits build routines, and a Pomodoro timer builds focus. TickTick bundles both, while Todoist offers neither.</p>

    <h3>TickTick Habits</h3>
    <p>TickTick has a built-in habit tracker that supports daily, weekly, and custom frequency habits, streak counts, and habit reminders. Habits appear alongside tasks in the today view, which keeps them top of mind. The free tier allows a limited number of habits, and Premium unlocks unlimited.</p>

    <h3>TickTick Pomodoro</h3>
    <p>TickTick has a built-in Pomodoro timer that starts when you tap a task. It logs focus sessions per task, so you can see how much actual work time a task took. The timer supports customizable work and break intervals and integrates with the time tracker.</p>

    <h3>Todoist Limitations</h3>
    <p>Todoist has no native habit tracker and no Pomodoro timer. Users who want habits must use recurring tasks as a workaround or integrate a third-party app like Habitica. For Pomodoro, users must run a separate timer app and manually log time. For users who want an all-in-one productivity system, this is a meaningful gap.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">TickTick</span> wins habits and Pomodoro.</p>

    <h2>Team Collaboration</h2>
    <p>If you work with a team, shared projects and roles matter. Todoist is built for teams, while TickTick is built for solo users.</p>

    <h3>Todoist Business</h3>
    <p>Todoist Business at $6 per user per month adds team shared projects with granular permissions, admin roles, activity logs, and project templates. You can assign tasks to specific team members, set deadlines, and track progress across the team. Todoist also integrates with Slack, Gmail, Google Calendar, and 80+ other tools, which makes it a natural fit for teams already using those apps.</p>

    <h3>TickTick Shared Lists</h3>
    <p>TickTick supports shared lists but not team projects. You can share a list with another user and assign tasks within that list, but there are no admin roles, no activity logs, and no project templates. For a couple sharing a grocery list, this is fine. For a team running a project, it is inadequate.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Todoist</span> wins team collaboration.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing depends on whether you are a solo user or part of a team. TickTick is cheaper for individuals, while Todoist is the only option for teams.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Todoist</th><th>TickTick</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>5 projects, 5 users per project</td><td>Unlimited tasks, limited lists</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Individual Pro</td><td>$4/mo ($36/yr)</td><td>$2.99/mo ($35.99/yr)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Team</td><td>$6/user/mo (Business)</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Calendar View</td><td>Pro and above</td><td>All tiers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Habit Tracker</td><td>Not available</td><td>Free limited, Premium unlimited</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pomodoro Timer</td><td>Not available</td><td>All tiers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Time Tracking</td><td>Not available</td><td>All tiers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI Features</td><td>AI Assist (Pro and above)</td><td>Limited</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Custom Themes</td><td>Pro and above</td><td>Premium</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For solo users, TickTick Premium at $2.99 per month offers significantly more features per dollar than Todoist Pro at $4 per month. You get calendar, habits, Pomodoro, and time tracking in one app. Todoist Pro gives you AI Assist, custom filters, and unlimited projects, but you need separate apps for habits and focus work.</p>
    <p>For teams, Todoist Business is the only viable option. TickTick does not offer a team tier, so if you need shared projects with roles and audit logs, Todoist is the choice regardless of price.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best solo value goes to <span class="winner-tag">TickTick</span>. Best team plan goes to <span class="winner-tag">Todoist</span>.</p>

    <h2>Platform Integration and Offline Support</h2>
    <p>Where the app lives and how it works offline matters as much as its features.</p>

    <h3>Integrations</h3>
    <p>Todoist integrates with 80+ apps including Slack, Gmail, Google Calendar, Outlook, Trello, Zapier, IFTTT, and Amazon Alexa. The Todoist API is well-documented and widely supported by third-party tools. TickTick integrates with Google Calendar, Outlook Calendar, and a handful of other apps, but its integration ecosystem is much smaller.</p>

    <h3>Offline Mode</h3>
    <p>TickTick has full offline support. You can create, edit, and complete tasks without internet, and changes sync when you reconnect. Todoist has limited offline support on web and desktop, with full offline only on mobile. For users who travel or work in low-connectivity environments, TickTick is more reliable.</p>

    <h3>Voice Assististants</h3>
    <p>Todoist has native integrations with Amazon Alexa, Google Assistant, and Siri, so you can add tasks by voice. TickTick has its own voice input in the mobile app but does not integrate with smart speakers natively.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best integrations go to <span class="winner-tag">Todoist</span>. Best offline support goes to <span class="winner-tag">TickTick</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Todoist Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Best natural language input in the category</li>
        <li>Strong team collaboration with Business plan</li>
        <li>80+ integrations including Slack, Gmail, and Google Calendar</li>
        <li>Clean, minimal interface that stays fast on large task lists</li>
        <li>AI Assist for breaking down tasks and generating sub-tasks</li>
        <li>Voice capture via Alexa, Google Assistant, and Siri</li>
        <li>Available on every major platform including Wear OS and Apple Watch</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Todoist Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>No native habit tracker</li>
        <li>No Pomodoro timer</li>
        <li>No time tracking</li>
        <li>Calendar view is basic compared to TickTick</li>
        <li>Limited offline support on web and desktop</li>
        <li>Pro plan is more expensive than TickTick Premium</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>TickTick Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>All-in-one app with tasks, calendar, habits, and Pomodoro</li>
        <li>Best calendar view of any task manager in 2026</li>
        <li>Built-in time tracking per task</li>
        <li>Full offline support across all platforms</li>
        <li>Cheaper premium tier at $2.99 per month</li>
        <li>Voice input on mobile</li>
        <li>Cross-platform including Linux and Wear OS</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>TickTick Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>No real team or business tier</li>
        <li>Smaller integration ecosystem</li>
        <li>Natural language parser less reliable than Todoist</li>
        <li>Interface can feel cluttered with all features enabled</li>
        <li>Limited AI features compared to Todoist AI Assist</li>
        <li>No native smart speaker integration</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Solo Professionals Who Want All-in-One</h3>
      <p>If you are a solo professional who wants tasks, habits, calendar, and focus timer in one app, TickTick is the better choice. You get more features for less money, and the all-in-one approach means you are not juggling three or four separate apps. Knowledge workers, students, and freelancers who value an integrated productivity system consistently prefer TickTick.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Teams and Small Businesses</h3>
      <p>If you work with a team and need shared projects, roles, and audit logs, Todoist is the only real choice. Todoist Business at $6 per user per month is purpose-built for team collaboration, while TickTick has no equivalent tier. Marketing teams, design studios, and small agencies that need to assign tasks and track progress across members should pick Todoist.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Minimalists Who Want Pure Task Capture</h3>
      <p>If you want the fastest, cleanest task capture and do not care about habits or Pomodoro, Todoist is the better choice. Its natural language input is unmatched, the interface stays out of the way, and the app stays fast even with thousands of tasks. Minimalists who value speed and simplicity over extra features consistently prefer Todoist.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Users Who Need Calendar and Time Blocking</h3>
      <p>If you plan your day hour by hour and want tasks on a calendar with drag-and-drop rescheduling, TickTick is the better choice. Its calendar view is the best of any task manager in 2026, and the time blocking workflow is integrated with tasks, habits, and the Pomodoro timer. Knowledge workers who live by their calendar consistently prefer TickTick.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Users in Low-Connectivity Environments</h3>
      <p>If you travel frequently or work in places with unreliable internet, TickTick is the better choice. Its full offline support lets you create, edit, and complete tasks without internet, and changes sync when you reconnect. Todoist has limited offline support on web and desktop, which makes it less reliable for travelers.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Freelancer managing 20 client projects</h3>
      <p>A freelance designer juggles 20 active client projects with deadlines, recurring check-ins, and habit-based routines like daily sketching. TickTick consolidates all of this into one app: client tasks live in folders, daily sketching is a habit with streaks, and the Pomodoro timer tracks actual work time per project. The designer opens one app in the morning and sees the full plan for the day including habits and time blocks. With Todoist, the designer would need Todoist for tasks, a separate habit app, and a separate Pomodoro timer, which adds friction.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Marketing team running a product launch</h3>
      <p>A five-person marketing team coordinates a product launch across blog posts, email campaigns, social media, and paid ads. Todoist Business assigns each channel to a team member, sets deadlines, and tracks progress in a shared project. The team lead sees the full project in a Kanban board and uses activity logs to audit who did what. TickTick cannot support this workflow because it has no real team tier with roles and audit logs.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Student planning a semester with habits</h3>
      <p>A university student plans a semester with assignment deadlines, daily study habits, and Pomodoro-based focus sessions. TickTick tracks assignments in a calendar view, daily review and exercise as habits with streaks, and study sessions with the Pomodoro timer. The student sees the full day in one screen: tasks by time block, habits to check off, and a focus timer ready to start. Todoist would require a separate habit app and timer, which fragments the workflow.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Executive capturing tasks from email and meetings</h3>
      <p>A busy executive captures tasks from email threads, meeting notes, and voice commands throughout the day. Todoist's natural language input and integrations with Gmail, Slack, and Siri let the executive add tasks without switching apps. Typing "Follow up with Sarah next Monday #sales" creates the task in the right project with the right due date in one line. TickTick supports similar input but is less reliable on complex recurrences and lacks deep email integration.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best overall for solo users who want all-in-one: TickTick.</strong> TickTick Premium at $2.99 per month bundles tasks, calendar, habits, Pomodoro, and time tracking in one app. For solo professionals, students, and freelancers who want a complete productivity system without juggling multiple apps, TickTick offers the best value and the most integrated experience.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for teams and collaboration: Todoist.</strong> Todoist Business at $6 per user per month is the only real choice for teams that need shared projects, roles, and audit logs. Combined with the best natural language input and 80+ integrations, Todoist is the natural default for marketing teams, design studios, and small businesses.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for fast capture and minimalists: Todoist.</strong> If you want the fastest, cleanest task capture and do not need habits or Pomodoro, Todoist Pro at $4 per month is the better choice. The natural language parser is unmatched, the interface stays out of the way, and the app remains fast with thousands of tasks.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For users who want both team collaboration and an all-in-one personal system, run Todoist Business for team projects and TickTick Premium for personal tasks, habits, and time blocking. Total cost is around $9 per month per user and covers every productivity scenario.</p>
    </div>

