    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is TickTick better than Todoist?</h3>
    <p>TickTick is better if you want an all-in-one app with calendar, habit tracking, Pomodoro timer, and time tracking built in. Todoist is better if you want the cleanest, fastest task manager with the best natural language input and team collaboration features. For solo users who value extras, TickTick wins. For teams and purists, Todoist wins.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Todoist or TickTick?</h3>
    <p>TickTick is cheaper. TickTick Premium costs $2.99 per month billed annually or $35.99 per year and includes all features. Todoist Pro costs $4 per month billed annually or $36 per year for individuals, and Todoist Business costs $6 per user per month. TickTick offers more features per dollar, while Todoist charges more for team features.</p>

    <h3>Does Todoist have a calendar view?</h3>
    <p>Yes, Todoist added a calendar view in 2024 for Pro and Business plans. It shows upcoming tasks in a monthly or weekly grid and integrates with Google Calendar and Outlook Calendar. However, TickTick's calendar is more full-featured, supporting drag-and-drop rescheduling, time blocking, and a dedicated agenda view that Todoist still lacks.</p>

    <h3>Can TickTick track habits?</h3>
    <p>Yes, TickTick has a built-in habit tracker that supports daily, weekly, and custom frequency habits, streak counts, and reminders. It is included in the free tier with limited habits and unlimited in Premium. Todoist does not have a native habit tracker and requires third-party integrations or workarounds like recurring tasks.</p>

    <h3>Which has better natural language input?</h3>
    <p>Todoist has the best natural language input in the category. Typing "Meeting every Friday at 2pm #work" creates a recurring task with the correct due date, time, and project in one line. TickTick supports similar input but is less reliable on edge cases like complex recurrences and natural language durations. For fast capture, Todoist wins.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Todoist and TickTick together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, but it adds friction. A common setup is Todoist for team projects and shared lists, plus TickTick for personal tasks, habits, and time blocking. The two apps do not sync natively, so tasks must be managed separately. Most users pick one to avoid duplication, with Todoist favored for teams and TickTick for solo productivity.</p>

